{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.82,"content_digest":"e832e1ebf176d92ce198818a5b70f285cf32edf6645f25759fa7951424cd8bc5","created_at":"2026-08-22T15:43:50+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"27eff02ca71b654d83660346561de989b70bd47ce55c9ea360ae84f7a363a581","input_tokens":5627,"latency_ms":58187,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"044deee93bde84350e9425197792862db4bb353a0a63a09bed7573bc7bc4a3dd","output_tokens":2320,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:5e0d7aa92107a406b70f68ff","meta_principle_id":"meta:neuroscience-cognition:attractor-dynamics","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0N277BZY4BEH9NZ6APH38PR","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim that statistically independent sensory objects become mapped to attractor relics in a transient-state network is a specific application of the general attractor-dynamics principle, which holds that recurrent interactions can create stable or metastable attractor patterns. The new principle depends on the existence and functional role of attractor relics while adding a particular input-mapping condition.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T15:43:50+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.75,"content_digest":"011b38552b7c309ea9cffb4ae994d9dd431cf1a81a18a4f13e6efa7b2341e8ba","created_at":"2026-08-23T04:24:57+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"6fee05f687c73735a8dd529fdc8ca1ce60a151af6d77609cbe11a419cb34af71","input_tokens":5655,"latency_ms":29912,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"fa80543c07f6ed688699997e68330b80178203471661e999ac27804778e1d024","output_tokens":2737,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:6ee95fb3f1efcdd0446632ba","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:data-quality-ceiling","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0PDSQMCV6YV777VXHWG1V8B","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The data-quality-ceiling principle states that model performance is bounded by information in data, which is consistent with the principle's condition that new data must provide information not already contained in existing data for epistemic uncertainty to be reduced.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:24:57+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.78,"content_digest":"6a9c208b34c69c9195e83948558860fc08e4c45b3004af4a8b17299a8f588541","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:58:44+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:f58a4e0a210a2ed012407bf3","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:data-quality-ceiling","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:general:01M0D8RASWSR3GMH7K4JG2AK3V","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that learning systems cannot reliably recover distinctions absent from observations and inherit coverage gaps. Limited experimental structure data for large-scale multimers is exactly such a coverage gap, making it harder for models to generalize to those assemblies. The specific condition of large-scale multimers is within the scope of the general data-quality ceiling, and the epistemic types are compatible: an empirical ML principle supporting a study-bound empirical claim.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:58:44+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
